---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import InstallCommand from '../components/InstallCommand.astro'
import WakeDemo from '../components/WakeDemo.astro'

const kinds = [
  {
    kind: 'postgres',
    title: 'Databases',
    body: 'Postgres 18 in a container, on a plain data directory. Unmodified, no extensions required, pg_dump always works.',
    wake: 'wakes on an inbound connection',
  },
  {
    kind: 'app',
    title: 'Apps',
    body: 'Any Dockerfile. Point hobby deploy at a directory, get a build and an HTTP route with TLS in front of it.',
    wake: 'wakes on an inbound request',
  },
  {
    kind: 'worker',
    title: 'Workers',
    body: 'Cloudflare-style Workers on workerd, with Durable Objects and their alarms mirrored outside the runtime.',
    wake: 'wakes on an inbound request',
  },
  {
    kind: 'queue',
    title: 'Queues',
    body: 'Durable messages with a consumer binding. The first thing here where stored state, not a connection, starts a container.',
    wake: 'wakes on a message arriving',
  },
]

const measurements = [
  { path: 'Postgres, wire protocol', p50: '170ms', p95: '186ms', box: 'Apple silicon laptop', when: '2026-08-07' },
  { path: 'HTTP app', p50: '121ms', p95: '133ms', box: 'Apple M5 Pro', when: '2026-08-10' },
  { path: 'Worker, workerd', p50: '299ms', p95: '321ms', box: 'Apple M5 Pro', when: '2026-08-10' },
]

const working = [
  { what: 'postgres', state: 'running', note: 'The reliable part. Cold start measured, eject verified end to end against real Docker.' },
  { what: 'app', state: 'running', note: 'Any Dockerfile, built and served. Cold start measured.' },
  { what: 'worker', state: 'running', note: 'Durable Object state survives a sleep, verified against real Docker rather than asserted.' },
  { what: 'eject', state: 'running', note: 'A docker-compose.yml and your data. The promise that makes the rest honest.' },
  { what: 'studio', state: 'starting', note: 'A web UI for tables, SQL and schema. Young, and network exposed by design. Put it behind Tailscale or a tunnel.' },
  { what: 'mcp', state: 'starting', note: 'Fourteen tools over the daemon API. Creating an app or worker from an agent is not wired yet.' },
]

const broken = [
  {
    what: 'queue producers',
    note: 'Sending a message from inside a container fails DNS on Linux. The consumer half works everywhere. Unimplemented, not untested: nothing passes the flag the container needs.',
    href: 'https://github.com/uziiuzair/hobbyist/blob/main/docs/queues/CLAUDE.md',
  },
  {
    what: 'caddy certificates',
    note: 'The certificate store is not persisted, so replacing the container re-issues certificates. That matters against Let’s Encrypt rate limits.',
    href: '/docs/guides/tls-and-domains/',
  },
  {
    what: 'ext4',
    note: 'Cheap copies need reflinks, and ext4 has none. It is the default image on a lot of the cheap VPS providers this is aimed at. Detected at hobby init, warned about rather than fatal.',
    href: '/docs/reference/filesystems/',
  },
]

const unbuilt = [
  { what: 'snapshots', note: 'Implemented and tested in the daemon, reachable from no command and no route. Use pg_dump until a verb exists.' },
  { what: 'branching', note: 'Copy-on-write project branches. Phase 1.5, designed and not built.' },
  { what: 'remote deploy', note: 'The CLI talks to a unix socket, so today it has to run on the daemon’s own box. Laptop to VPS needs its own decision record first.' },
  { what: 'object storage', note: 'S3-compatible storage and volumes for compute. Phase 3.' },
]
---

<Base
  title="Hobbyist: your stack, your box, their convenience"
  description="A self-hosted platform that feels like Neon and Supabase, on hardware you own. Everything sleeps when nothing is using it, and wakes when something connects. v0-alpha."
>
  <!-- 1. Hook, and the thesis in one panel -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero__grid">
      <div>
        <p class="hero__tag"><b>v0-alpha</b> · not production ready · not a business</p>
        <h1 class="h-display">
          Ten projects,<br />one small box,<br /><em>all of them asleep.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="lede hero__lede">
          A self-hosted platform that feels like Neon and Supabase, on hardware you own.
          Everything sleeps when nothing is using it, and wakes when something connects.
          One command, one box, no Kubernetes.
        </p>
        <div class="hero__cta">
          <InstallCommand />
          <p class="hero__note">
            Ubuntu, Debian or macOS. Needs Docker. Installs Bun for you.{' '}
            <a href="/docs/install/">What it does</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <WakeDemo />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Who this is for -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <blockquote class="quote">
        <p>
          With the rise of AI came a lot of new hobbyists, people who build stuff for
          themselves. Hobby is for them. Deploy it on your own server. Mine runs on my home
          server, reachable through Tailscale and a Cloudflare Tunnel, and it hosts my
          demo environments.
        </p>
        <footer>The reason this exists, written to a friend before it had a website</footer>
      </blockquote>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          If you have a home server, an old ThinkPad, or a five dollar VPS, and a folder of
          half-finished projects that each want a database, this is for you. The problem was
          never that Postgres is hard to run. It is that running ten of them costs ten times
          as much attention and ten times as much memory as running one, and nine of them are
          idle at any moment.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. The problem -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="state state--sleeping">the gap</span>
        <h2 class="h-section">You are already paying for convenience, not capability</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Every primitive underneath a managed platform is free and mature. Postgres is free.
          Containers are free. Caddy gets you TLS for nothing. The convenience is what people
          pay for, and it is the only thing missing from the self-hosted world.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What managed platforms sell</h3>
          <p>
            Instant provisioning. Branching. Scale to zero. A connection string that just
            works. A dashboard that makes a database legible. A deploy that happens on push.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What self-hosting gives you</h3>
          <p>
            All of the primitives, none of the feel. A compose file you wrote once and no
            longer remember, running at full cost whether or not anything is using it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. The wedge -->
  <section class="section" id="wedge">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="state state--awake">the wedge</span>
        <h2 class="h-section">Everything sleeps, and everything wakes on demand</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Sleep is what makes ten projects fit on one small box. Wake is what makes sleep
          invisible. Neither is useful without the other, and the pair is the whole product.
          It is also the one thing no self-hostable alternative does.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid--2">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Self-hosted Supabase never sleeps</h3>
          <p>
            A large multi-container stack that runs at full cost whether or not anything is
            using it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Xata&rsquo;s open plugin cannot wake</h3>
          <p>
            In their own words, it &ldquo;can&rsquo;t handle reactivation because the cluster
            is no longer running once it&rsquo;s hibernated.&rdquo; Reactivation on connect is
            what they kept in the paid cloud.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Coolify and Dokploy do not sleep apps</h3>
          <p>They deploy well, and everything they deploy keeps running.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Hobbyist holds your connection open</h3>
          <p>
            A proxy speaking the Postgres wire protocol starts the container, waits for the
            database to accept queries, then finishes the handshake. Your client sees one
            slightly slow connection. It never sees an error.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. The numbers, including the one that does not exist -->
  <section class="section" id="cold-start">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="state state--starting">cold start</span>
        <h2 class="h-section">Under a second, or it does not count</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The budget is 1 second target and 3 seconds hard ceiling. Three seconds is roughly
          where common ORM and pool connect timeouts start firing, so anything above it is a
          release blocker rather than a slow path.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table class="data">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Path</th>
              <th scope="col">p50</th>
              <th scope="col">p95</th>
              <th scope="col">Measured on</th>
              <th scope="col">When</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              measurements.map((m) => (
                <tr>
                  <th scope="row">{m.path}</th>
                  <td class="num">{m.p50}</td>
                  <td class="num">{m.p95}</td>
                  <td class="dim">{m.box}</td>
                  <td class="dim num">{m.when}</td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="assume">
        <h3>The measurement that does not exist</h3>
        <p style="margin:0">
          Every number above came from a laptop, which is the easy end of the matrix.
          <strong>The five dollar VPS has never been measured</strong>, and it is the machine
          this project is aimed at. Treat the table as an existence proof that the approach
          works, not as a promise about your hardware. Filing a measurement from a cheap VPS
          is the single most useful contribution available right now.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. What is in the box -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="state state--sleeping">four kinds</span>
        <h2 class="h-section">A project is a namespace holding typed resources</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Adding a kind means implementing one interface and adding one line to a registry.
          That is why later phases add to this list instead of rewriting it. Durable Objects
          are a capability of a worker, not a fifth kind.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid--4">
        {
          kinds.map((k) => (
            <div class={`card card--${k.kind}`}>
              <code class="card__kind">{k.kind}</code>
              <h3>{k.title}</h3>
              <p>{k.body}</p>
              <div class="card__wake">{k.wake}</div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Status, told honestly, tagged with the product's own states -->
  <section class="section" id="status">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="state state--failed">status</span>
        <h2 class="h-section">What works, and what is openly broken</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          This is v0-alpha. Postgres is the part that is reliably configured and working.
          Everything below is stated so you can decide correctly whether to run it, which is
          the only thing this page is for.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="h-sub" style="margin-bottom:0.9rem">Working</h3>
        <ul class="status">
          {
            working.map((row) => (
              <li data-state={row.state}>
                <span class="status__what">{row.what}</span>
                <span class="status__note">{row.note}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="h-sub" style="margin-bottom:0.9rem">Known broken</h3>
        <ul class="status">
          {
            broken.map((row) => (
              <li data-state="failed">
                <span class="status__what">{row.what}</span>
                <span class="status__note">
                  {row.note} <a href={row.href}>Details</a>.
                </span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="h-sub" style="margin-bottom:0.9rem">Not built yet</h3>
        <ul class="status">
          {
            unbuilt.map((row) => (
              <li data-state="undeployed">
                <span class="status__what">{row.what}</span>
                <span class="status__note">{row.note}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p class="checked">
          <a href="/docs/status/">The full status page</a> keeps this list current and links
          each item to the file that records it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Leaving -->
  <section class="section" id="leaving">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="state state--sleeping">the exit</span>
        <h2 class="h-section">You can always leave</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          This is the first promise and the one that makes the rest honest. The data directory
          is a plain Postgres data directory. Nothing is a proprietary format, no extension is
          required, and Postgres itself is unmodified.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="install" style="max-width:38rem">
        <div class="install__cmd">
          <span class="install__prompt" aria-hidden="true">$</span>
          <code>hobby eject blog --release</code>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          What you get is a directory you can <code>docker compose up</code> on any machine
          with Docker, with no Hobbyist installed anywhere. That is the whole exit. There is no
          export queue, no support ticket and no thirty day window.
          <a href="/docs/guides/eject/">How eject works</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Not a business -->
  <section class="section" id="not-a-business">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="section__head">
        <span class="state state--sleeping">no pricing page</span>
        <h2 class="h-section">Nobody is expected to pay</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          There is no cloud offering, no hosted tier, no paid feature, no metering and no
          billing, and no roadmap toward one. Nothing is held back behind a paid guard.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is load-bearing rather than a disclaimer. It removes multi-tenancy, isolation
          hardening, usage accounting and quota enforcement from scope, and those removals are
          what make the project buildable by one person. It is also why there is no warranty:
          nothing here ships whose failure mode is someone else losing data quietly, and what
          does ship is labelled.
        </p>
        <p>
          The success metric is that the author is still using it daily in six months. Stars,
          forks and issue count are noise.
          <a href="/compare/">See how it compares, and what it costs to run</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <InstallCommand />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
